date: 2026-05-17 (UTC)
host: lilith primary dev rig (Ryzen 9 7950X + RTX 5070)
git: jxl-encoder-gpu@HEAD (sibling workspace `jxl-encoder-gpu--patches-afv-preservation`)
parent: jxl-encoder-gpu/main @ commit 406b40bb (W7-3 auto-AFV-on-screenshots)
command:
  cargo run --release --features 'cuda encoder' \
    --example afv_preservation_diagnostic -- --distance 1.0

What the readout proves:
- "fired" column: whether `find_and_build_patches` returned `Some` AND
  the case-1 recompute ran (line ~2120 / ~2639 in encoder.rs).
- "afv_pre": GPU AFV first-block count BEFORE the recompute (mirrors
  what the GPU strategy-search plan + auto-AFV-on-screenshots dispatch
  produced).
- "afv_post": AFV first-block count AFTER the CPU
  `compute_ac_strategy` recompute. When `fired=true`, this is the
  CPU's independent AFV count on patches-subtracted XYB; when
  `fired=false`, this equals `afv_pre` (no recompute).
- "afv_lost": `max(0, afv_pre - afv_post)`. ALL ZERO across the
  10-image corpus — proves the W7-3 commit's premise was inverted.

Key finding (chunk 1 conclusion):

  The CPU `compute_ac_strategy` recompute does NOT discard GPU AFV picks.
  It runs independently on patches-subtracted XYB and produces its
  OWN AFV picks — typically MORE than the GPU produced (terminal:
  40 → 214; windows: 264 → 449; imessage: 0 → 791). The DCT8 column
  shows a corresponding decrease (~12,000 fewer DCT8 blocks across
  the 10 images), indicating the CPU recompute is reassigning DCT8
  blocks to AFV / IDENTITY / other small transforms — not just
  "wiping" the GPU plan but performing a fresh, more thorough
  strategy search on patches-subtracted XYB.

  The W7-3 commit message ("preserving GPU AFV picks across the
  patches case-1 recompute would unlock the picks currently wiped on
  terminal/windows (40 + 264 picks)") is technically true (the GPU
  picks themselves don't survive) but operationally FALSE — those
  GPU picks are not load-bearing on bitstream bytes because the CPU
  recompute already picks AFV on those blocks plus many more.

  This explains the W7-3 sweep result: terminal.png + windows.png
  saved 0 bytes when auto-AFV was turned ON. The GPU AFV picks the
  dispatch produced on those images had no bytes impact because the
  patches case-1 recompute generates equivalent or better AFV
  assignment from scratch. The 0.091% sweep-wide saving came
  exclusively from the patches-NOT-detected subset (gmessages,
  graph, gui), where the GPU plan flows through unmodified to the
  bitstream emit path.

Implications for chunk 2:
- The "preserve GPU AFV across patches recompute" follow-on is NOT
  worth pursuing. The CPU recompute is already doing the work.
- A separate, real wedge exists: the auto-AFV dispatch on screenshots
  produces no incremental win on patches-fired images (terminal,
  windows). The byte saving could be larger if the W7-3 gate
  (`mask1x1 median > 95 + e>=7`) were dropped entirely on the
  patches-detected screenshot subset — because the CPU recompute
  evaluates AFV unconditionally at e>=6, the GPU dispatch is
  effectively dead code on those images. Worth a follow-on chunk:
  measure whether disabling auto-AFV on patches-detected screenshots
  speeds up the GPU strat-search without bytes loss.
- The unconditional CPU recompute (`compute_ac_strategy` on
  patches-subtracted XYB, ~1100 ms at 5.6 MP imac_dark in this run)
  is the dominant time cost on patches-fired screenshots. A real
  perf wedge is GPU-porting `compute_ac_strategy` for that path —
  but that's item #4 in the V2 audit
  (`vardct_gpu_dropped_optimizations_resurrection_2026-05-17.md`),
  multi-week scope, not chunk-2.

Filed: chunk 1 closes the "preserve GPU AFV picks" investigation
as misdirected. Diagnostic instrumentation retained for future
audits.
